'''Henderson''' will be a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]], returning a single member to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. A former electorate from 1969 to 1978 and again from 1993 to 1996, it has been recreated with new boundaries for the [[2026 New Zealand general election|2026 general election]].

==Population centres==
Henderson is based in [[West Auckland, New Zealand|West Auckland]] and provides representation for the communities of [[Henderson, New Zealand|Henderson]], [[Henderson Valley]], [[Lincoln, Auckland|Lincoln]], [[Massey, New Zealand|Massey]], [[Rānui]] and [[Swanson, New Zealand|Swanson]].<ref name=":0">{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=12 September 2025 |page=18 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

The electorate is roughly triangular and borders the electorates of [[Kaipara ki Mahurangi]] and [[Upper Harbour (New Zealand electorate)|Upper Harbour]] in the north, [[Glendene (electorate)|Glendene]] in the east, and [[Waitakere (electorate)|Waitakere]] on the southwest. 

== History ==

=== 1969 to 1978 ===
Henderson was first created through the 1967 electoral redistribution which saw the creation of three additional electorates in the [[North Island]] and one additional electorate in the [[South Island]]. One of those new electorates was Henderson, which took over most of {{NZ electorate link|Waitakere}}'s area. These changes came into effect with the {{NZ election link|1969}}.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=106, 111}} Localities covered by the electorate included [[Henderson, New Zealand|Henderson]], [[Oratia]], [[Waiatarua]], [[Parau]], [[Huia, New Zealand|Huia]], and [[Piha]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}} In the 1972 electoral redistribution, there were slight boundary adjustments with the adjoining {{NZ electorate link|Waitemata}} and {{NZ electorate link|New Lynn}} electorates.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=111, 115}} 

Henderson existed for three electoral cycles, all of which were won by [[Martyn Finlay]] of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], who had represented the former Waitakere electorate since 1963. Henderson was abolished again through the 1977 electoral redistribution. Much of its population were transferred to the restored [[Waitakere (electorate)|Waitakere electorate]].{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=115, 119}}

=== 1993 to 1996 ===
[[File:Henderson electorate, 1993.png|thumb|200px|Henderson electorate boundaries between 1993 and 1996]]Henderson was recreated for the {{NZ election link|1993}} and existed for one parliamentary term. In [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]], the first [[Mixed-member proportional representation|mixed-member proportional]] (MMP) election, the area was absorbed into the new [[Waipareira|Waipareira electorate]].

=== From 2026 ===
The 2025 boundary review saw the reconfiguration of electorates in West Auckland resulting in the disestablishment of [[Te Atatū (electorate)|Te Atatū]], [[Kelston (New Zealand electorate)|Kelston]] and [[New Lynn (New Zealand electorate)|New Lynn]]. The restored Henderson electorate took population from parts of Te Atatū ([[Swanson, New Zealand|Swanson]], [[Rānui]], [[Western Heights, Auckland|Western Heights]], [[Henderson, New Zealand|Henderson]] and [[Lincoln, Auckland|Lincoln]]), [[Upper Harbour (New Zealand electorate)|Upper Harbour]] ([[Massey, New Zealand|Massey]]) and [[Kaipara ki Mahurangi]] (Birdwood Heights). The original proposal for the name of the electorate was Rānui.<ref name=":0" />

